{
 "name": "Schrödinger two qubits (product and Bell)",
 "description": "Two qubits, pictured classically: each 2×2 grid of wells (blue) is left/right for qubit 1 and top/bottom for qubit 2, so the four wells are the four combinations 00, 01, 10, 11. The potential is a double well across plus a double well down, so the two qubits tunnel independently, as if each had its own gate. Left: power in the top row, a product state (qubit 2 is \"top\", qubit 1 is an even mix): the whole row tunnels down and back together. Right: power in top-left and bottom-right only, the shape of a Bell state, the classic entangled pair: it can't be written as a column pattern times a row pattern. It swaps to the other diagonal and back, but never becomes a row or a column: whatever qubit 1 does, qubit 2 does too (steps that act on one qubit at a time can never untangle it, or tangle a product state). What a classical field can't do: here both \"qubits\" are features of one wave in one place, so there is no spooky distance; and n qubits would need 2ⁿ wells (4 here, about a million for 20), the cost real qubits don't pay.",
 "rule": "builtin:8",
